  "verbalization": "Myricetin appears to exert its biological effects through a series of target-mediated pathways that ultimately lead to interactions with HEp-2 cells. The evidence suggests that Myricetin acts as a target for multiple compounds, including Macluraxanthone B, Longistyline C, and 5-Hydroxymethyl-9-methoxycanthin-6-one, all of which are implicated in targeting HEp-2 cells. These compounds are likely involved in cellular processes that influence the behavior of HEp-2, potentially through mechanisms such as enzyme inhibition or signaling pathway modulation. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Myricetin in modulating cellular functions in HEp-2, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The evidence supports a network of interactions where Myricetin indirectly influences HEp-2 through its targets, suggesting a broader role in cellular regulation.",
